WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
(pensive music)

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.120
- [Narrator] These lines are Taiwan's

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.460
14 underwater internet cables

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.910
that connect the island
to the rest of the world.

00:00:08.956 --> 00:00:10.440
(booming sound)

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.360
In August, China held military drills

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.890
in these six zones on top

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.410
of some of this critical infrastructure.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.260
And while Beijing hasn't said
these cables are a target,

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.810
China's increasing military
activity in the region

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.480
is putting this vulnerable
connection at risk.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.670
- If the communication was not able

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:27.990
to function very well

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.143
during the war time that
will become a disaster.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.780
- [Narrator] So here's
how Taiwan's internet

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:34.770
is under threat

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.470
and what the island is doing
to beef up and defend it.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.930
Submarine cables are bundles

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.850
of fiber optic lines about the thickness

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.200
of a garden hose.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.630
98% of international internet traffic

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.330
goes through them and the
cables surrounding Taiwan play

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:50.880
a critical role in connecting
the Asia-Pacific region

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:51.840
to the web.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.618
- North Asia to South East Asia

00:00:54.618 --> 00:00:57.900
either goes through
Taiwan or nearby ocean.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:01.200
A lot of country will
certainly be impacted

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.870
if Taiwan cable has a issue.

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:04.770
- [Narrator] Kenny Huang runs

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.870
the Taiwan Network Information Center,

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:08.610
a nonprofit that works with the government

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.400
to coordinate cybersecurity
research and response.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.560
- Everything rely on the internet.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.900
So the importance of submarine,

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.957
just like the blood to our body.

00:01:18.957 --> 00:01:20.940
- [Narrator] But this
infrastructure is precarious

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.940
even when there's no
military activity nearby.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.710
Fishing and other maritime activity

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.880
can damage the cables,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.640
especially in the busy seas around Taiwan.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.320
And natural disasters like earthquakes

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.550
or typhoons are capable

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.340
of disabling multiple cables at once.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.440
That happened in December 2006

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.780
when an earthquake in
Taiwan cut several cables,

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.230
drastically restricting
communications across Asia.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.490
- During the earthquake,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.440
I remember telecommunication company,

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.890
they are only able to
maintain some portion

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.270
of their voice call.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.899
The data communication was
very poorly maintained.

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:56.520
(whooshing sound)

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.590
- [Narrator] And security
analysts say this infrastructure

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.120
could be particularly vulnerable

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.940
to being damaged or disabled on purpose.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:06.330
- If there's a conflict
between Taiwan and China,

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.750
and they are very high
possibility the initial target

00:02:10.753 --> 00:02:12.750
could be a cable landing station.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:15.030
- [Narrator] These dots are
Taiwan's landing stations,

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.100
the places where a
submarine cable connects

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.660
to a local network.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.580
The island has four of these locations

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.310
and their were focal points
during a series of war games run

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:24.723
by a US think tank.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.170
Last year, it explored what
Chinese military action

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.690
in the region around
Taiwan would look like.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.640
In these war games, Chinese teams targeted

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:33.900
Taiwan's landing stations

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.550
with missiles and cyber attacks,

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.320
which cut off the internet in Taiwan

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.010
and severely limited traffic
for Japan, Guam and Hawaii.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.930
Each time, China gained the upper hand

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.520
because Taiwan's team was forced

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.710
to shift its focus away from defense

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.540
to restoring its ability to communicate

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.863
with the outside world.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.770
To repair any damaged infrastructure,

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.690
Taiwan would need help
from private companies

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:56.640
and nearby countries like Japan.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.770
But security analysts say any assistance

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.860
would be unlikely amid military activity.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.120
Just take a look at what happened

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.020
to the Trans-Pacific Express
during China's recent drills.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.730
On July 25th, this cable,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.610
which connects Taiwan to other
countries suffered a failure

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:13.620
off the north coast of the island.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.490
About a week later,

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:18.090
a Japanese cable repair company
dispatched a ship to fix it

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.997
but three days later, China's
military exercises began

00:03:20.997 --> 00:03:23.910
and the ship was unable
to fix the connection.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:28.080
- It's questionable if
any maintenance operator

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.550
willing to repair a cable

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.130
because in the war time,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.320
I believe that would
be very tough for them

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.870
to do that kind of maintenance.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.020
- [Narrator] China's State
Council Information Office

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.380
didn't respond to a request for comment

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:41.790
about whether Beijing considers

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.160
Taiwan's cable infrastructure a target

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.910
and if it would allow repairs
during military activity.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.800
So Taiwan is looking to protect itself

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.960
by adding more connections
to the internet,

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.090
like this new cable called Apricot,

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:55.860
which has funding from Meta, Alphabet

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:59.280
and telecom operators in Taiwan,
Japan and the Philippines.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.980
It's due to start service in 2024.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.660
And alongside laying more cables,

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:06.930
Taiwan is also looking to
create a backup with this.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:10.200
Low Earth satellite systems
like SpaceX's Starlink

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.580
can provide internet access

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.717
without any physical
connections over land or sea.

00:04:14.717 --> 00:04:16.890
During a visit to SpaceX's headquarters

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.180
in California in July,

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.910
Taiwan's Minister of
Science and Technology said

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.160
the island was committed
to continuing its close

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.890
and mutual cooperation with the company.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.470
Taiwan's Minster of Digital
Affairs said in a statement

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.440
that the island's internet
is a national security issue

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.060
and the government is allocating funds

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.170
to multiple solutions,

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.890
including 5G base stations
and satellite connectivity.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.240
But integrating these options

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.150
into Taiwan's communication
systems could take years.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.950
- Satellite communication is not something

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.180
like a plug and play.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:49.505
We need to make sure when we
need it, it will be in place.

00:04:49.505 --> 00:04:50.820
- [Narrator] China,
which sees Taiwan as part

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:53.190
of its territory has vowed
to take back the island

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.900
by force if necessary.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.700
As the risk of a conflict escalates,

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.610
the internet is just one of
many strategic resources,

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.290
as well as ports and manufacturing

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.483
that Taipei is stepping up to protect.

